well the last minute announcement by the  provincial government to return to a  modified step two of the reopening plan  has left many parents and business  owners baffled by the sudden decision  and frustrated by the mixed messages  that sentiment is the driving force  behind this op-ed published in the  toronto sun with a headline that reads  as you can see ontarians are locked down  again and that it's unacceptable  and the author post media columnist  anthony fury joins us now anthony we  appreciate you joining us so let's talk  about your headline you say it is  unacceptable why is that  well you know angie for the past 24  hours i have heard the frustrations of  so many people who say hold on a second  we were fine to go along with previous  lockdowns but now we've been vaccinated  some of us have been triple vaccinated  we followed all the rules we postponed  seeing family members we downloaded that  qr code app so we could comply with the  vaccine passport our children many of  them have been vaccinated and now here  we are facing a variant that public  health ontario has now confirmed is  significantly milder and yet we feel  like we're back to square one and i  think a lot of people are just very  frustrated with that and they're saying  we've had enough  so when you look at schools and  businesses referring to schools for  example they've said all along they are  last to close first to open  last week the chief medical officer of  health dr kieran moore said he saw no  reason to make changes to the student's  planned return and that was to be on  monday so what happened between then and  now  well that's a really good question  because there's been a lot of nuance and  context that's often missed with the  conversation around kids in covid  pediatric infectious diseases experts  have said uh one of the silver linings  that there can be one of this whole  pandemic is they found that covet 19 has  proven to be less severe than influenza  when it comes to kids  we've got tony fauci and others stepping  forward to say that uh the reported rise  in pediatric hospitalizations that's not  really what's going on that so much of  this is actually incidental  hospitalizations meaning kids are coming  in for broken arms just happening to  test positive for asymptomatic proven  and in ontario the reporting on cases in  schools isn't really what it looks like  because when we report these numbers  cases in schools people take that to  mean transmission of the virus happening  in school but as dr kieran moore has  affirmed as dr eileen davila as dr  lawrence loebs they have all maintained  the actual in-class transmission has  been a very small percentage of that  number so there's a lot of data that  that could be assuring parents uh if it  got out there more that i don't think  we're hearing amplified enough so let's  talk about the politics behind the  scenes to really lead to this decision  of course and also looking ahead to five  months from now and how much of an  impact that will have with the  provincial election what are your  thoughts  yeah that's a very good question and  unfortunately i think politics is  playing a role in this i think one  appreciates that teachers unions  certainly uh want to look out for the  safety and best interests of the people  they represent but at the same time when  you saw various teachers unions uh  posting online the past few days that  they did want to push for delayed school  reopenings they would have hashtags that  said vote for it out or what have you  sort of political sloganeering and one  goes well hold on a second are we  actually talking exclusively about uh  concerns around covet 19 and  transmission in the classroom or are we  also talking about a bit of a political  play going on here and i think if  there's an element of the latter going  on and it appears there is that's uh  incredibly unfortunate i think parents  would be right to be pretty frustrated  that uh perhaps part of the delay in  their kids returning to school has been  exacerbated by political reasons all  right so we don't have a lot of time but  you you've heard the other argument they  say look we can't just  you know miraculously create  all these healthcare workers to deal  with a possible search not to mention  the the current situation what are your  thoughts on that  yeah i think we've been hearing this for  a long time and i would encourage people  to look at the ontario sunshine list  where they say that we have hundreds of  people who are paid uh 200 300 400 600  000 a year to effectively manage the  hospital system i'm not talking about  our frontline heroes i'm talking about  back office administrators who are not  doctors and we've thrown hundreds of  billions of dollars at covet 19 and  people are saying hold on a second two  years into this we're still told that  children  who are not at serious risk of covet 19  have to lose out and be denied uh in  class schooling because you can't find a  way to manage the system such that you  can't manage a search capacity that  right now is about 240 persons in icu  and that's very frustrating to people  well certainly a lot of frustration and  confusion we appreciate this anthony  fury a columnist with post media always  a pleasure sir thank you  thank you
 

